{
{#- Model-family logo (unitysvc/unitysvc-data#60): the catalog row shows the
    model's own mark rather than the provider's — the model is what a customer
    recognises, and the provider is already named beside it. Resolved from the
    model id, so no per-model parameter is needed and the populate script
    cannot destroy it. A model the registry does not know resolves to null,
    which creates no document and leaves the provider logo in place, so the key
    needs no conditional. -#}
  "logo": {{ {"$logo_preset": offering_name} | tojson }},
  "name": "{{ offering_name }}",
  "display_name": "{{ display_name }}",
{#- summary = marketplace teaser (<=200 chars, required by unitysvc#1838):
    first paragraph of the param description. description keeps the remaining
    paragraphs; a single-paragraph description is left unchanged. -#}
{%- set _paras = (description or (display_name ~ ', served through the UnitySVC gateway.')).split('\n\n') %}
{%- set _summary = (_paras[0] | trim)[:200] %}
{%- set _description = (_paras[1:] | join('\n\n')) if _paras | length > 1 else _paras[0] %}
  "summary": {{ _summary | tojson }},
  "description": {{ _description | tojson }},
  "service_type": "{{ service_type | default('llm') }}",
  {#- Platform capability vocabulary (unitysvc docs/capabilities.yml): every
     model here is a chat service — a conversation in, generated text out. -#}
  "capabilities": {{ capabilities | default(['chat']) | tojson }},
  "status": "{{ status | default('ready') }}",
  "currency": "USD",
{#- No `tags` key. unitysvc#1962 closes the tag vocabulary (docs/tags.yml) and
    ingest now REJECTS anything outside it, which retires all four values this
    template used to declare: `ai` restated service_type, `gateway` restated
    gateway_type (DERIVED so it cannot drift), and `byok`/`managed` restated
    the enrollment channels the gateway actually routes on. Those two channel
    NAMES survive as the keys of upstream_access_config below -- that is the
    routing contract, and only the tag values are gone.

    The one tag a seller may declare, `feature:func-call`, is deliberately NOT
    set here. It is a per-model fact and this repo has no per-model signal for
    it: AionLabs' /v1/models returns id, name, description, date,
    context_length, max_completion_tokens, is_moderated, architecture and
    pricing, and nothing about tool support -- there is no field for
    scripts/update_params.py to capture. Asserting it for the catalog instead
    would be a guess covering a roleplay finetune (aion-rp-llama-3.1-8b)
    alongside the Aion models, and a wrong guess is customer-visible: the
    service advertises tool calling and the shipped example comes back
    `400 invalid request: tool use is not supported by the provided model`.
    Under-claiming is recoverable, so these offerings stay untagged until
    AionLabs exposes the fact per model (or it is verified per model by hand).

    Rendering no key at all is equivalent to an empty list here -- ingest's
    compose_service_tags replaces the seller-owned half from each upload -- so
    the legacy tags are cleared on the next upload rather than left behind. #}
{#- Formats are a service-level fact, identical for every AionLabs model: an
    OpenAI-compatible upstream, exposed in both dialects via the llm_translator
    below (callers may send OpenAI or Anthropic; responses come back in kind).
    Merge them into the per-model details rather than repeating in every param. -#}
{%- set _details = details.copy() %}
{%- set _ = _details.update({"input_formats": ["openai", "anthropic"], "output_formats": ["openai_response", "anthropic_response"]}) %}
  "details": {{ _details | tojson }},
{%- if payout_price %}
  "payout_price": {{ payout_price | tojson }},
{%- endif %}
{#- AionLabs resells its own tokens, so both channels front the same upstream and
    differ only in whose key pays: managed = seller key (${ secrets.* }),
    byok = customer key (${ customer_secrets.* }). Channel TYPE is derived from
    that alone (no flag). llm_translator lets an OpenAI upstream also serve
    Anthropic-style callers; the gateway auto-detects and only translates when
    the incoming dialect differs. -#}
  "upstream_access_config": {
    "managed": {
      "access_method": "http",
      "api_key": "${ secrets.{{ env_api_key_name }} }",
      "base_url": "{{ api_base_url }}",
      "rate_limits": {{ rate_limits | default([]) | tojson }},
      "routing_key": {"model": "{{ details.model_name }}"},
      "request_transformer": {"llm_translator": "anthropic_to_openai"},
      "sort_order": 1
    },
    "byok": {
      "access_method": "http",
      "api_key": "${ customer_secrets.{{ env_api_key_name }} }",
      "base_url": "{{ api_base_url }}",
      "rate_limits": {{ rate_limits | default([]) | tojson }},
      "routing_key": {"model": "{{ details.model_name }}"},
      "request_transformer": {"llm_translator": "anthropic_to_openai"},
      "sort_order": 2
    }
  }
}
